{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumnCollision","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumnCollision","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Young.YoungDiagram","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.TwoRowDimCatalan","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Intervals"],"declarations":38,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_agrees_with_decided_shapes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_agrees_with_decided_shapes","kind":"theorem","line":439,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L439-L450","doc":"**Cross-check: the two routes meet.**\n\n`twoCol k m` is *asserted* to be the transpose of `(k+m, k)`; that assertion is\nwhere the general proof could silently be about the wrong shape while still\ntypechecking. These evaluate `twoCol` at the three sizes the decided cases cover\nand confirm it produces exactly the lists those cases used — so\n`dimHLF_twoCol 2 2 : dimHLF (twoCol 2 2) = dimHLF [4, 2]` and the decided\n`dimHLF [4, 2] = dimHLF [2, 2, 1, 1]` are statements about one shape, not two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.shape_sets_differ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.shape_sets_differ","kind":"theorem","line":428,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L428-L437","doc":"**The shape sets are different**, as lists, at `n = 3` and `n = 6`.\n\nWorth stating next to the equal totals: it is the conjunction of \"different sets\"\nwith \"same total\" that is dangerous, and a theorem asserting only one half\ninvites the reader to supply the wrong other half. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sq_sum_agree_six","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sq_sum_agree_six","kind":"theorem","line":415,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L415-L426","doc":"The same at `n = 6` — `A = 2` in the mass channel — where the two families\nshare **nothing at all** and the totals still agree. The disjoint case is the\nsharper one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sq_sum_agree_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sq_sum_agree_three","kind":"theorem","line":408,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L408-L413","doc":"Both readings total `catalan 3 = 5`, over sets sharing only `(2,1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dim_eq_dim_transpose_three_to_six","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dim_eq_dim_transpose_three_to_six","kind":"theorem","line":393,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L393-L406","doc":"Transposed shapes have equal dimension at `n = 3, 4, 5, 6`, by evaluation.\n\nNote `(2,1)` at `n = 3` and `(2,2)` at `n = 4` are **self**-conjugate — they lie\nin *both* families. That is why the two shape sets overlap rather than being\ndisjoint, and it is what makes the collision hard to notice by inspection. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_two_col_sq_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_two_col_sq_sum","kind":"theorem","line":368,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L368-L382","doc":"**`Σ dim² = catalan n` over the ≤ 2-COLUMN family, at every `n`.**\n\n`QOU.Mathlib.TwoRowDimCatalan.dimHLF_two_row_sq_sum` had this for the ≤ 2-row\nfamily only. The two families are different sets (`shape_sets_differ`) and this\nsays their squared-dimension totals agree at every `n`, not just where they were\ndecided — so the dimension check provably cannot separate the conventions, at\nany size. That is the trap stated as a theorem instead of as a warning. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_two_col_eq_two_row","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_two_col_eq_two_row","kind":"theorem","line":349,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L349-L366","doc":"**`dim λ' = dim λ` for the two-row/two-column family, at every `n`.**\n\nThis is the lemma the row half and the column half of the corpus needed in order\nto be about each other. `QOU.MathlibExt.WenzlSchurElement` and\n`QOU.MassTheory.SelfConjugateStratumVanishes` stratify by `μ.rowLen 0 ≤ 2` (at\nmost two columns); `QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower` and\n`QOU.QBeta.TwoRowNormalisation` stratify by two rows. Combined with\n`transposeEquiv` above — which matches the *shapes* — this matches the\n*dimensions*, so a statement in either convention now transports.\n\nIt is **not** the general `dim λ = dim λ'`: nothing here touches shapes with\nthree or more rows *and* three or more columns. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_twoCol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.dimHLF_twoCol","kind":"theorem","line":343,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L343-L347","doc":"**`dim (2^k 1^m) = dim (k+m, k)`** — the transposed shapes have equal\nFrame–Robinson–Thrall dimension. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sz_twoCol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.sz_twoCol","kind":"theorem","line":339,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L339-L341","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoCol_eq_twoRow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoCol_eq_twoRow","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L328-L337","doc":"**The two hook products are equal.**\n\nNot by a factorial computation on each side — the three factors of\n`prodHooks_twoCol` and `prodHooks_twoRow` are *termwise the same*, under\n`a = k+m`, `b = k`. Only the association differs, so `ring` closes it. This is\nthe whole reason the general statement is cheap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoRow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoRow","kind":"theorem","line":299,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L299-L326","doc":"**The hook product of the two-row shape**, split at the column where the\nsecond row runs out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoCol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_twoCol","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L278-L297","doc":"**The hook product of the two-column shape**, split at the row where the\nrows of length two run out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.prodHooks_eq","kind":"theorem","line":272,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L272-L276","doc":"`prodHooks` as a `Finset` product. Definitionally the same object; the\n`Finset` form is what the interval-splitting API works on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoRow_colLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoRow_colLen","kind":"theorem","line":266,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L266-L270","doc":"Column lengths of the two-row shape `(a, b)`: column `j` meets row `0` when\n`j < a` and row `1` when `j < b`. Unconditional — `b ≤ a` is not needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_getD_ge","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_getD_ge","kind":"theorem","line":258,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L258-L264","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_getD_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_getD_lt","kind":"theorem","line":252,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L252-L256","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_colLen_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_colLen_one","kind":"theorem","line":248,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L248-L250","doc":"Column `1` meets only the rows of length two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_colLen_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_colLen_zero","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L244-L246","doc":"Column `0` of `2^k 1^m` meets every row. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_length","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol_length","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L241-L242","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.twoCol","kind":"def","line":237,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L237-L239","doc":"The two-column shape `2^k 1^m`: `k` rows of two cells, then `m` rows of one.\nIt is the transpose of the two-row shape `(k+m, k)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.families_differ_at_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.families_differ_at_three","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L206-L213","doc":"**Both facts in one statement**, at the smallest size where they differ:\nthere is a shape in each family that the other misses, and both have three\ncells — so a size-graded argument cannot separate them either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.exists_atMostTwoCols_not_atMostTwoRows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.exists_atMostTwoCols_not_atMostTwoRows","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L196-L204","doc":"**The transposed witness**, `(1,1,1)`: at most two columns, more than two\nrows. So neither family contains the other — the failure is symmetric, not an\nartefact of which side the witness was built on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoRows_iff_atMostTwoCols_false","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoRows_iff_atMostTwoCols_false","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L187-L194","doc":"**The two conditions are not equivalent.**\n\nStated as the refutation of the universally quantified biconditional, because\nthat is the claim an agent transporting a theorem between the two halves of the\ncorpus is implicitly making. It is false, and `row3` is why. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_not_atMostTwoCols","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_not_atMostTwoCols","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L184-L185","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_atMostTwoRows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_atMostTwoRows","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L181-L182","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_card","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L179-L179","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_colLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_colLen","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L170-L177","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_rowLen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3_rowLen","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L161-L168","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.mem_row3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.mem_row3","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L157-L159","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.row3","kind":"def","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L148-L155","doc":"The single row `(3)`: three cells, all in row `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.transposeEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.transposeEquiv","kind":"def","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L121-L137","doc":"**The two families are in explicit bijection at every size.**\n\nThe map is transposition, and it is the *only* correct transport between the\nconventions: it is a bijection, and — by `atMostTwoRows_iff_atMostTwoCols_false`\nbelow — it is not the identity, so a family-level statement does not survive\nbeing read in the other convention just because the counts match. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.card_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.card_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L111-L115","doc":"**Transposing preserves the number of cells**, so it is a bijection of\npartitions of `n` and not merely of diagrams. Mathlib has `transpose` as an\norder isomorphism but records nothing about `card`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoRows_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoRows_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L105-L109","doc":"**…and back.** The mirror of `atMostTwoCols_transpose`, so transport works in\nboth directions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoCols_transpose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.atMostTwoCols_transpose","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L96-L103","doc":"**Transposing sends the row condition to the column condition.**\n\nThis is the lemma `WenzlSchurElement`'s docstring asks for. A theorem proved for\n`μ.rowLen 0 ≤ 2` applies to a two-row shape only after transposing it — not to\nthe two-row shape itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.instDecidableAtMostTwoCols","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.instDecidableAtMostTwoCols","kind":"instance","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L91-L92","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.instDecidableAtMostTwoRows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.instDecidableAtMostTwoRows","kind":"instance","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L88-L89","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.AtMostTwoCols","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.AtMostTwoCols","kind":"def","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L83-L86","doc":"**At most two columns** — the convention of `WenzlSchurElement` and\n`SelfConjugateStratumVanishes`. The number of columns of `μ` is the length of\nits zeroth row. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.AtMostTwoRows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.TwoRowTwoColumn.AtMostTwoRows","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/TwoRowTwoColumnCollision.lean#L78-L81","doc":"**At most two rows** — the convention of `TemperleyLiebTower`,\n`TwoRowNormalisation`, and convention D's surviving Wedderburn blocks. The\nnumber of rows of `μ` is the length of its zeroth column. ","signatureChunk":0}]}